{"name":"Cottingley Fairies","hex":"#eddbd7","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"fairies","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of fairy","plural of fairie"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fairies"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cottingley Fairies","blocks":[{"text":"The Cottingley Fairies are the subject of a hoax which purports to provide evidence of the existence of fairies. They appear in a series of five photographs taken by Elsie Wright and Frances Griffiths, two young cousins who lived in Cottingley, near Bradford in England. In 1917, when the first two photographs were taken, Elsie was 16 years old and Frances was 9. The pictures came to the attention of writer Sir Arthur Conan Doyle, who used them to illustrate an article on fairies he had been commissioned to write for the Christmas 1920 edition of The Strand Magazine. Doyle was enthusiastic about the photographs, and interpreted them as clear and visible evidence of supernatural phenomena. Public reaction was mixed; some accepted the images as genuine, others believed that they had been faked."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/7/71/Cottingley_Fairies_1.jpg/330px-Cottingley_Fairies_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cottingley_Fairies"},{"title":"Fairy","blocks":[{"text":"A fairy is a type of mythical being or legendary creature, generally described as anthropomorphic, found in the folklore of multiple European cultures, a form of spirit, often with magical, metaphysical, supernatural, or preternatural qualities."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Falero_Luis_Ricardo_Lily_Fairy_1888.jpg/330px-Falero_Luis_Ricardo_Lily_Fairy_1888.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fairy"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/d/d2/CottingleyFairies-PrincessMary2_gobeirne.png/500px-CottingleyFairies-PrincessMary2_gobeirne.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/d/d2/CottingleyFairies-PrincessMary2_gobeirne.png/500px-CottingleyFairies-PrincessMary2_gobeirne.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Comparison of Cottingley Fairies and illustrations from Princess Mary's Gift Book","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACottingleyFairies-PrincessMary2_gobeirne.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/CottingleyBeck.jpg/330px-CottingleyBeck.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/CottingleyBeck.jpg/330px-CottingleyBeck.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cottingley Beck, where Frances and Elsie claimed to have seen the fairies","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACottingleyBeck.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/c/c9/CottingleyFairies2.jpg/500px-CottingleyFairies2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/c/c9/CottingleyFairies2.jpg/1280px-CottingleyFairies2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/c/c9/CottingleyFairies2.jpg/500px-CottingleyFairies2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The second of the five photographs, showing Elsie with a winged gnome","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACottingleyFairies2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/f/f9/CottingleyFairies3.jpg/330px-CottingleyFairies3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.or